/-
# The Boltzmann Bridge X — Local-to-Global Gluing of Interleaving Geodesics

Bridge IX (`Applications.BoltzmannBridge.InterleavingGeodesic`) exhibited the
convex-interpolation path `lerp F G t := (1−t)·F.weight + t·G.weight` as a
*constant-speed geodesic* of the interleaving metric, proving the linear law

> `eInterleavingDist (lerp F G s) (lerp F G t) = ofReal |s − t| · eInterleavingDist F G`
> (`eInterleavingDist_lerp`).

Bridge X adds the **local-to-global** layer that turns this single path into a
self-coherent *sheaf of geodesics*.  The decisive structural fact is

> **`lerp (lerp F G s) (lerp F G t) r = lerp F G ((1−r)·s + r·t)`**
> (`lerp_lerp`),

i.e. the geodesic between two points *on* the geodesic `F ⟶ G` is again the
**same** geodesic, merely reparametrised by the affine map `r ↦ (1−r)s + rt`.
This is the gluing axiom: the global geodesic is determined by, and consistent
with, its restriction to every subinterval.  From it we read off the genuinely
*metric* consequences:

* `eInterleavingDist_lerp_right` — distance from `lerp F G t` to the far endpoint
  `G` is `ofReal (1 − t) · d(F, G)` (the mirror of Bridge IX's
  `eInterleavingDist_lerp_left`).
* `eInterleavingDist_lerp_betweenness` — for `s ≤ u ≤ t` the three points are in
  exact metric *betweenness*: `d(s,u) + d(u,t) = d(s,t)`.  The triangle
  inequality is realised as an **equality** all along the path.
* `eInterleavingDist_lerp_bisect` — every interior point splits the total distance
  additively: `d(F, lerp t) + d(lerp t, G) = d(F, G)` for all `t ∈ [0,1]`.  This
  generalises Bridge IX's `eInterleavingDist_midpoint` (the case `t = ½`) from a
  single point to the whole continuum.
* `eInterleavingDist_lerp_lerp` — speed is *multiplicative* under reparametrisation:
  a sub-geodesic of a sub-geodesic scales the base distance by the product of the
  two parameter gaps.

## Main results

* `lerp_lerp` — the affine self-similarity / gluing law of the geodesic.
* `eInterleavingDist_lerp_right` — distance to the far endpoint.
* `eInterleavingDist_lerp_betweenness` — exact additive betweenness for `s ≤ u ≤ t`.
* `eInterleavingDist_lerp_bisect` — additive split at every interior point.
* `eInterleavingDist_lerp_lerp` — multiplicativity of geodesic speed under nesting.
-/
import Mathlib
import Applications.BoltzmannBridge.HigherPersistence
import Applications.BoltzmannBridge.PersistenceStability
import Applications.BoltzmannBridge.BottleneckStability
import Applications.BoltzmannBridge.InterleavingMetric
import Applications.BoltzmannBridge.InterleavingClosure
import Applications.BoltzmannBridge.InterleavingIsometry
import Applications.BoltzmannBridge.InterleavingGeodesic

open Finset BigOperators
open scoped ENNReal

namespace BoltzmannBridge

namespace Filtration

variable {α : Type*}

/-! ## The gluing law: a geodesic of geodesic-points is the same geodesic -/

-- !-- Expand both inner `lerp` weights and the outer one via `lerp_weight`; the
-- !-- coefficient of `F` is `(1−r)(1−s)+r(1−t) = 1−((1−r)s+rt)` and of `G` is
-- !-- `(1−r)s+rt`, so `ext_weight`/`ring` close it.  The new endpoint parameter
-- !-- `(1−r)s+rt` is a convex combination of `s,t ∈ [0,1]`, hence again in `[0,1]`. -- !--
/-- **The affine self-similarity (gluing) law of the geodesic.**  Interpolating
between two points `lerp F G s` and `lerp F G t` of the geodesic `F ⟶ G` returns the
*same* geodesic, reparametrised by the affine map `r ↦ (1−r)·s + r·t`.  This is the
local-to-global coherence: the global geodesic restricts consistently to every
subsegment. -/
theorem lerp_lerp (F G : Filtration α) {s t r : ℝ}
    (hs0 : 0 ≤ s) (hs1 : s ≤ 1) (ht0 : 0 ≤ t) (ht1 : t ≤ 1)
    (hr0 : 0 ≤ r) (hr1 : r ≤ 1) :
    lerp (lerp F G s hs0 hs1) (lerp F G t ht0 ht1) r hr0 hr1
      = lerp F G ((1 - r) * s + r * t)
          (by nlinarith [mul_nonneg (sub_nonneg.mpr hr1) hs0, mul_nonneg hr0 ht0])
          (by nlinarith [mul_le_mul_of_nonneg_left hs1 (sub_nonneg.mpr hr1),
                mul_le_mul_of_nonneg_left ht1 hr0]) := by
  apply ext_weight; funext σ
  simp only [lerp_weight]
  ring

/-! ## Distance to the far endpoint -/

-- !-- `G = lerp F G 1` (`lerp_one`), so the distance is `d(lerp t, lerp 1) =
-- !-- ofReal |t − 1| · d`; `|t − 1| = 1 − t` since `t ≤ 1` (`abs_sub_comm`,
-- !-- `abs_of_nonneg`). -- !--
/-- **Distance from an interior point to the far endpoint `G`.**  The mirror image of
Bridge IX's `eInterleavingDist_lerp_left`: `d(lerp F G t, G) = ofReal (1 − t) · d(F, G)`. -/
theorem eInterleavingDist_lerp_right (F G : Filtration α) {t : ℝ}
    (ht0 : 0 ≤ t) (ht1 : t ≤ 1) :
    eInterleavingDist (lerp F G t ht0 ht1) G
      = ENNReal.ofReal (1 - t) * eInterleavingDist F G := by
  have h : eInterleavingDist (lerp F G t ht0 ht1) G
      = eInterleavingDist (lerp F G t ht0 ht1) (lerp F G 1 zero_le_one le_rfl) := by
    rw [lerp_one]
  rw [h, eInterleavingDist_lerp,
      show |t - 1| = 1 - t from by rw [abs_sub_comm]; exact abs_of_nonneg (by linarith)]

/-! ## Exact metric betweenness along the geodesic -/

-- !-- Rewrite all three distances by `eInterleavingDist_lerp`; with `s ≤ u ≤ t` the
-- !-- gaps are `|s−u| = u−s`, `|u−t| = t−u`, `|s−t| = t−s`.  Pull the scalar out
-- !-- (`add_mul`), merge the two `ofReal` halves (`ENNReal.ofReal_add`), and
-- !-- `(u−s)+(t−u) = t−s` by `ring`. -- !--
/-- **Exact additive betweenness.**  For `s ≤ u ≤ t` in `[0,1]` the three geodesic
points satisfy `d(s,u) + d(u,t) = d(s,t)`: the triangle inequality holds as an
*equality*, witnessing that `lerp` traverses the metric on a straight, non-wasteful
path. -/
theorem eInterleavingDist_lerp_betweenness (F G : Filtration α) {s u t : ℝ}
    (hs0 : 0 ≤ s) (hsu : s ≤ u) (hut : u ≤ t) (ht1 : t ≤ 1) :
    eInterleavingDist (lerp F G s hs0 (by linarith)) (lerp F G u (by linarith) (by linarith))
      + eInterleavingDist (lerp F G u (by linarith) (by linarith)) (lerp F G t (by linarith) ht1)
      = eInterleavingDist (lerp F G s hs0 (by linarith)) (lerp F G t (by linarith) ht1) := by
  rw [eInterleavingDist_lerp, eInterleavingDist_lerp, eInterleavingDist_lerp,
      show |s - u| = u - s from by rw [abs_sub_comm]; exact abs_of_nonneg (by linarith),
      show |u - t| = t - u from by rw [abs_sub_comm]; exact abs_of_nonneg (by linarith),
      show |s - t| = t - s from by rw [abs_sub_comm]; exact abs_of_nonneg (by linarith),
      ← add_mul, ← ENNReal.ofReal_add (by linarith) (by linarith)]
  congr 2
  ring

/-! ## Additive splitting at every interior point -/

-- !-- `d(F, lerp t) = ofReal t · d` (`eInterleavingDist_lerp_left`), `d(lerp t, G) =
-- !-- ofReal (1−t) · d` (`eInterleavingDist_lerp_right`); merge the halves to
-- !-- `ofReal (t + (1−t)) · d = ofReal 1 · d = d`. -- !--
/-- **Additive split at every interior point.**  For all `t ∈ [0,1]`, the point
`lerp F G t` lies exactly on the metric segment from `F` to `G`:
`d(F, lerp F G t) + d(lerp F G t, G) = d(F, G)`.  This generalises Bridge IX's
`eInterleavingDist_midpoint` (the case `t = ½`) to the full continuum of interior
points. -/
theorem eInterleavingDist_lerp_bisect (F G : Filtration α) {t : ℝ}
    (ht0 : 0 ≤ t) (ht1 : t ≤ 1) :
    eInterleavingDist F (lerp F G t ht0 ht1)
      + eInterleavingDist (lerp F G t ht0 ht1) G
      = eInterleavingDist F G := by
  rw [eInterleavingDist_lerp_left, eInterleavingDist_lerp_right,
      ← add_mul, ← ENNReal.ofReal_add ht0 (by linarith),
      show t + (1 - t) = 1 from by ring, ENNReal.ofReal_one, one_mul]

/-! ## Multiplicativity of geodesic speed under reparametrisation -/

-- !-- Rewrite both nested `lerp`s by the gluing law `lerp_lerp` to land on
-- !-- `lerp F G` of affine parameters, apply `eInterleavingDist_lerp`, factor the
-- !-- parameter gap `((1−a)s+at) − ((1−b)s+bt) = (a−b)(t−s)`, and split the absolute
-- !-- value and `ofReal` multiplicatively (`abs_mul`, `ENNReal.ofReal_mul`). -- !--
/-- **Speed is multiplicative under nesting.**  A sub-geodesic of the sub-geodesic
`lerp F G s ⟶ lerp F G t`, traversed from parameter `a` to `b`, has interleaving
length equal to the product of the two parameter gaps times the base distance:
`d = ofReal |a − b| · (ofReal |s − t| · d(F, G))`.  Constant speed composes by
multiplication of rates — the hallmark of a self-similar geodesic field. -/
theorem eInterleavingDist_lerp_lerp (F G : Filtration α) {s t a b : ℝ}
    (hs0 : 0 ≤ s) (hs1 : s ≤ 1) (ht0 : 0 ≤ t) (ht1 : t ≤ 1)
    (ha0 : 0 ≤ a) (ha1 : a ≤ 1) (hb0 : 0 ≤ b) (hb1 : b ≤ 1) :
    eInterleavingDist
        (lerp (lerp F G s hs0 hs1) (lerp F G t ht0 ht1) a ha0 ha1)
        (lerp (lerp F G s hs0 hs1) (lerp F G t ht0 ht1) b hb0 hb1)
      = ENNReal.ofReal |a - b| * (ENNReal.ofReal |s - t| * eInterleavingDist F G) := by
  rw [lerp_lerp, lerp_lerp, eInterleavingDist_lerp,
      show ((1 - a) * s + a * t) - ((1 - b) * s + b * t) = (a - b) * (t - s) from by ring,
      abs_mul, abs_sub_comm t s, ENNReal.ofReal_mul (abs_nonneg _), mul_assoc]

end Filtration

/-
-- !-- Lab Notebook -- !--

## Hypothesis
Bridge IX proved `lerp` is a constant-speed geodesic via the linear law
`eInterleavingDist (lerp s) (lerp t) = ofReal |s − t| · d` but treated the path as a
single object.  The adversarial hypothesis: the geodesic is *self-coherent* in the
sheaf sense — its restriction to any subinterval is again the geodesic of the
restricted endpoints, and consequently every interior point realises the triangle
inequality as an exact equality (betweenness), not merely an upper bound.

## Result
Confirmed.  The gluing law `lerp_lerp` shows that interpolating between two geodesic
points `lerp s`, `lerp t` reproduces `lerp F G` reparametrised by the affine map
`r ↦ (1−r)s + rt` — a single `ext_weight`/`ring` computation, with the new endpoint
parameter automatically convex (hence in `[0,1]`).  From it: the far-endpoint
distance `eInterleavingDist_lerp_right`, the exact betweenness
`eInterleavingDist_lerp_betweenness` for `s ≤ u ≤ t`, the universal additive split
`eInterleavingDist_lerp_bisect` (generalising the midpoint bisection to all `t`), and
the multiplicative speed law `eInterleavingDist_lerp_lerp`.

## Insight
Local-to-global coherence is *algebraic*: the geodesic is the image of the affine
line under one linear map (convex combination of weights), and affine maps compose to
affine maps — that single fact is `lerp_lerp`, and every metric corollary follows by
linearity of `ENNReal.ofReal` on the nonnegative parameter gaps.  Betweenness is thus
not an extra geometric input but the additive structure of `[0,1]` transported across
the Bridge VIII isometry.  The geodesic is a *sheaf of segments* whose gluing is
matrix-free: only the endpoints and a scalar parameter are ever needed.

## Failure analysis
The convexity hypotheses `0 ≤ · ≤ 1` are load-bearing throughout: outside `[0,1]` the
reparametrised endpoint can leave the valid-filtration locus, breaking `lerp_lerp`'s
target.  Betweenness requires the *ordering* `s ≤ u ≤ t`; without it the gaps no longer
add (`|s−u| + |u−t| > |s−t|`), and indeed only the triangle *inequality* survives — the
equality is special to monotone parameters.  Uniqueness of the geodesic is still open:
because `eInterleavingDist` is a `⨆` over simplices, non-maximising coordinates may
detour freely, so `lerp` is one section of a convex family of geodesics (see
FUTURE_DIRECTIONS).
-/

end BoltzmannBridge
